#!/usr/bin/env python3

# Converts a console mod - an xdelta/BPS/IPS patch against the stock ROM, or an
# already patched ROM - into a mod directory the port can mount with --moddir.
#
# A patched ROM cannot just be dropped in place of the stock one. The port runs
# decomp code, not the ROM's MIPS code, and it reads the ROM at the fixed
# segment offsets baked into port/src/romdata.c - offsets that stop matching the
# moment a mod changes the ROM's layout. What does carry over is the mod's
# content: the asset files it replaces or adds, and the segments it rebuilds.
#
# So this diffs the patched ROM against the stock one and writes out only what
# differs, in the layouts romdata.c and mod.c already load:
#
#   <out>/files/<rom filename>  asset file replacements and additions
#   <out>/segs/<segment name>   segment replacements
#   <out>/textures/%04x.bin     individual texture replacements
#   <out>/IMPORT.txt            what was imported, and what could not be
#
# What the port cannot use is set aside rather than dropped: files a mod built
# for its own copy of the game code, in files.incompatible/, and segments whose
# place in the patched ROM could not be established, in segs.unlocated/.
#
# The game does the same thing itself for a patch dropped into mods/:
# port/src/modimport.c is a port of this script, function for function, with
# port/src/rompatch.c in place of xdelta3. A change here wants making there too,
# and the two IMPORT.txt reports for one patch should read the same.

